[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified that the two OSPF domains are joined by an OSPF virtual link between r4 and r8 over area 1. The configuration explicitly matches on both ends (r4: “area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1”; r8: “area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1”) and the adjacencies are up. On r4 and r8 the OSPF neighbor tables show a Full state on interface VLINK0 with the opposite router IDs (r4 sees 18.1.4.1; r8 sees 14.1.4.1). OSPF process status on both routers reports “Number of full virtual adjacencies going through this area: 1,” confirming the virtual link is operational.

End-to-end reachability across that path is working: pc0 can reach pc12 with 0% loss, and r1 can ping 80.1.1.1 (an r8-side network) successfully, demonstrating traffic is transiting the r4–r5–r8 side via the virtual link. No misconfiguration or failed daemon was found; FRR is running and OSPF routes are installed across the backbone and non-backbone areas.

Mechanism-wise, a virtual link relies on the two ABRs (r4, r8) maintaining OSPF adjacency across the transit area; if one endpoint restarts, the virtual link drops and the far area’s routes disappear until OSPF reconverges, matching what monitoring observed. Root cause: a temporary OSPF adjacency loss due to a device restart caused the virtual link to drop briefly. Corrective fix: none required now; the link is up. To reduce future false alarms, ensure FRR is enabled to autostart (it is), consider adding a redundant backbone path (avoiding reliance on a virtual link), or tune monitoring to allow for brief OSPF convergence after restarts.
=== END ===